<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Eleutherna</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.) · capital Eleutherna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hill city of western Crete on the slopes of Mount Ida whose Orthi Petra cemetery has revealed rich warrior burials of the early Iron Age.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Eleutherna occupied a long ridge on the north-west slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ida-crete">Mount Ida</a>, inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. It grew into a city in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and by the 7th century BCE it was one of the important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Excavations at the Orthi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> necropolis since 1985 have found rich burials of the 9th to 7th centuries BCE, including warriors and elite women with gold jewellery. Like other Cretan cities, Eleutherna had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosmoi">kosmoi</a> magistrates and aristocratic institutions. It minted coins showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and took part in Hellenistic alliances and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cretan-koinon">Cretan Koinon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Metellus</a> captured Eleutherna during the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in 68 or 67 BCE. It continued as a Roman and early Byzantine town before being abandoned, probably after an earthquake in the 7th century CE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eleutherna.jpg" alt="Eleutherna" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Petr Novak · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleutherna_Bridge,_Crete,_Greece._Pic_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eleutherna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cretan Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-434&amp;focus=eleutherna" aria-label="Eleutherna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5305 901 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eleutherna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5625 1157L5632 1166M5625 1157L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5632 1166h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5625 1157h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5625 1157h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-434&amp;focus=eleutherna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

